<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Samtskhe</h1><p class="st-meta">1268 CE – 1628 CE · capital Akhaltsikhe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A south-west Georgian principality ruled by the Jaqeli atabegs, which broke from the Georgian crown and was finally absorbed by the Ottoman Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a>, the upland borderland around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhaltsikhe">Akhaltsikhe</a>, was governed by the Jaqeli family as royal lieutenants. In 1268 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargis-i-jaqeli">Sargis I Jaqeli</a> secured direct recognition from the Mongol <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, making his lands effectively separate from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia</a>, and his heirs held the title of atabeg.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The atabegs ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhaltsikhe">Akhaltsikhe</a> over farming valleys, the cave monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardzia">Vardzia</a> and routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beka-i-jaqeli">Beka I Jaqeli</a> issued a law code in the early 14th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a> rejoined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> but broke away again in the 15th century, becoming a battleground between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> after 1500 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from 1578.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Jaqeli rulers converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and in 1628 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a> into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/childir-eyalet">Childir Eyalet</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safar-pasha">Safar Pasha</a>, a Jaqeli, as its first governor. Much of the population was gradually Islamised, and the region returned to Georgian rule only after the Russian conquest of 1829.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-samtskhe.jpg" alt="Principality of Samtskhe" width="320" height="183"><figcaption>Image: Ercwlff · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Principality_of_Samtskhe.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1268 CE – 1628 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Akhaltsikhe</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sargis I Jaqeli, Beka I Jaqeli, Qvarqvare II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Georgian Orthodox Christianity, later Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Georgian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Samtskhe-Saatabago, Atabegate of Samtskhe, Meskheti</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1448&amp;focus=principality-of-samtskhe" aria-label="Principality of Samtskhe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5727 694 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Samtskhe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6047 950L6036 930M6047 950L6134 1224M6047 950L5709 970M6047 950L6054 966"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6036 930h0M6134 1224h0M5709 970h0M6054 966h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6047 950h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6047 950h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Akhaltsikhe. <a href="/atlas?year=1448&amp;focus=principality-of-samtskhe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
